cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or data, please cite it as below."
title: "When the Detector Becomes the Attack Surface: Explanation-Mediated Prompt Injection against Grounded LLM Log Analysts"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Abdullaev
    given-names: Bakhtier
    affiliation: "University of Debrecen, Faculty of Informatics"
  - family-names: Nguyen
    given-names: The Hoang Tung
    affiliation: "University of Debrecen, Faculty of Informatics"
repository-code: "https://github.com/tungthnguyen-dev/tdk-log-injection"
version: data-freeze-v1.2
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23547264
abstract: >-
  An LLM log analyst reads a web-server log event and decides whether it is an attack. Attackers control
  request fields such as the User-Agent, Referer and URI, so they can hide prompt-injection text in the log.
  We study a hybrid pipeline in which the LLM is also shown the verdict, attack probability and SHAP
  explanation of a Random Forest detector, so the same attacker-written field reaches the LLM both directly
  and through the detector evidence (explanation-mediated injection). The repository holds the isolated
  testbed, the detector, 1,440 injected cases, the pre-registration, every LLM result row from eight models,
  and the analysis scripts that reproduce all results.
keywords:
  - prompt injection
  - large language models
  - log analysis
  - security operations
  - SHAP
  - explainable AI
  - pre-registration
